  and the test does:

  [36mrequire[39m([31m"../settings.js"[39m);                          [32m// side effect: sets globalThis.AppSettings[39m
  [34mconst[39m { resolveEnvironment } = globalThis.AppSettings;

  The global [3mis[0m the interface, and the test consumes the same interface the browser does. No export tail, no environment
  sniffing. The one adjustment: eager resolution of [38;5;153mcurrent[39m is guarded by whether [38;5;153mlocation[39m exists, so requiring the
  file in Node doesn't throw.

  [1mThe [38;5;153mfile://[39m case — I need your call.[0m When [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is opened by double-click, [38;5;153mlocation.hostname[39m is the empty string,
  not [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m. Under fail-loudly as specified, that's an unmapped host: the page renders, but the login submit
  refuses and logs the error. You picked the classic-script form partly to keep double-click loading working, so I don't
  want to quietly break half of it.

  The clean fix is one more line in the hostname table — [38;5;153m"": "development"[39m — making "opened from disk" an explicit
  environment mapping rather than an accident. It preserves fail-loudly for every [3mreal[0m unknown host, because an empty
  hostname can only mean a local file.
